---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BuyButton from '../components/BuyButton.astro';
import Newsletter from '../components/Newsletter.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the Author"
  description="Varna Sri Raman wrote and illustrated The Very Last Superhero. Her personal site is idealog.works."
>
  <div class="shell section">
    <header class="page-head stack">
      <p class="eyebrow">Author and illustrator</p>
      <h1>{site.author}</h1>
      <p class="text-muted lede">
        She wrote this book and drew it. Both halves matter here — the illustrations are not
        decoration commissioned afterwards, and the afterword is explicit about it: <em
          >they are not illustrations, they are records</em
        >.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose author stack">
      <h2>Who she is</h2>
      <p>
        Varna Sri Raman is a development economist and licensed social justice worker. She holds a
        post-doctoral qualification in school innovations from Harvard and a PhD from the Tata
        Institute of Social Sciences, and is a Dr Manmohan Singh Fellow. She is
        Managing Director of Pinpoint Ventures Advisory and a co-founder of ImpactMojo and
        <a href="https://storywell.in" rel="noopener">StoryWell Books</a>, the independent Indian
        press that published this novel.
      </p>
      <p>
        Her subject is measurement, policy and power — unpaid care, budget allocations for informal
        workers, welfare schemes and who they actually reach — written about from the inside. She
        writes at <a href="https://policygrounds.press" rel="noopener">PolicyGrounds.press</a>, and
        carries bylines across the Indian press, among them the
        <a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/blogs/author/varnasriraman/" rel="noopener"
          >Times of India</a
        > and <a href="https://thewire.in/author/varna-sri-raman" rel="noopener">The Wire</a>.
        <a href="https://idealog.works" rel="noopener me">idealog.works</a> is the fuller record.
      </p>
      <p>
        The economics and the fiction are not separate careers. This novel is set in 2075 and
        argues about heat, waste, water and who is left carrying the cost of all three; that is the
        same argument, made in a form that lets a fourteen-year-old in Pune pick it up.
      </p>

      <h2>Forthcoming</h2>
      <p>
        <em>The Measurement Trap: India's Quest for Social Progress Through Eight Decades</em>,
        from Routledge. It reads India's statistical systems — the census, the sample surveys, the
        national accounts, 1947 to 2027 — as political institutions, and argues that what a state
        measures, it governs, and what it leaves uncounted, it leaves unattended. There is a
        companion site at <a href="https://whatcounts.in" rel="noopener">whatcounts.in</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Her other books</h2>
      <p>
        She illustrated all of these. They are children's books from
        <a href="https://storywell.in" rel="noopener">StoryWell</a>, and all of them are on Amazon.
        Each one carries the things StoryWell puts in every title — word lists, activities, pages
        to draw on, and notes for the teacher or parent reading it aloud.
      </p>
      <dl class="books">
        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/9395373016" rel="noopener"
            ><em>Bulbuls and the Big Big Breeze</em></a
          > — by Papri Sri Raman
        </dt>
        <dd>
          A pair of bulbuls decide to build their first ever nest in a rather unsafe location. A storm
          knocks it down — and what happens to the babies is a heart-warming story of human and
          animal kindness.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/9395373172" rel="noopener"><em>Happy Lemon</em></a> — by
          Papri Sri Raman
        </dt>
        <dd>
          A citrusy, lemony book. Join Lima as she shares her fresh tangy self, from the tree to an epic
          summer-cooler — an introduction to the joys of the humble Indian lemon.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/9395373156" rel="noopener"
            ><em>Kalyani's Ant Problem</em></a
          > — written with Papri Sri Raman
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Geometry taught through kolam. Kalyani and her grandmother chase ants across the
          threshold and rediscover the shapes in a form that was already doing the maths, with the
          ecology of the practice explained alongside it. For five to ten.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/9395373091" rel="noopener"><em>Rhythm Taal</em></a> — by
          Papri Sri Raman
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Musical instruments and the sounds they make, told from the point of view of a music-loving
          baby. A story of discovery and fun, and a way into sound and music for the very young.
        </dd>

        <dt>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.in/dp/9395373059" rel="noopener"><em>Roar!</em></a> — by Papri
          Sri Raman
        </dt>
        <dd>
          A small tiger cub goes through the forest meeting creatures great and small. Sounds and
          movement for young kids, in warm earthy colour — an easy Sunday read.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>This book</h2>
      <p>
        <em>The Very Last Superhero</em> is a speculative novel set in a climate-collapsed India of
        2075, published by {site.publisher} in {site.edition.toLowerCase()}. It begins after the era
        of superheroes rather than during it, and its argument is that what survives collapse is not
        power but memory — held by ordinary people, animals, salvaged tech and archivists who did
        not stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/making">On the Making</a> is her own account of why she wrote it, printed in the
        book and reproduced here in full. It is the best short answer to what the novel is for.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        Her personal site is <a href="https://idealog.works" rel="noopener me">idealog.works</a>.
        This site is about the book; that one is about her.
      </p>

      <h2>What is real in it</h2>
      <p>
        The Okhla waste-to-energy plant exists. The heat exists. The book is dedicated in part to
        someone fighting Delhi's waste-to-energy plants, and <a href="/act">How to Help</a> points
        at the people doing that work now.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/contact">Write to her directly</a> — about the book, a correction, a reader
        submission, or anything on this site that looks wrong.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="buy">
      <BuyButton />
      <p class="text-muted">
        {site.publisher} · {site.edition} · ISBN {site.isbn}
      </p>
    </aside>

    <Newsletter />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .lede,
  .author {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .books {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .books dt {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-m);
  }

  .books dd {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .books dd:empty {
    display: none;
  }

  .author h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .buy {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-l);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .buy p {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
